// Grow-on-demand pool policy (#2593, epic #1911's chosen design —
// `./pool-sizing.md`). Boot starts ONE worker; this file decides when a
// second, third, ... becomes worth its own OS thread, so a program that
// never has parallel work never pays for a pool it never uses.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE HARD CEILING: `schedMaxWorkers` (`./sleep.bit`), THE SAME ONE
// `schedStartWorker` (#2592, `./worker.bit`) ALREADY REFUSES PAST
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This file never starts a worker `schedStartWorker` would not have started
// anyway — `schedMaybeGrow` re-checks `scNWorkers < schedMaxWorkers` itself
// only so a full pool skips the lock `schedStartWorker` would otherwise take
// and immediately release; the ceiling is enforced exactly once, by
// `schedStartWorker` itself (`worker.bit:241`), the load-bearing check.
// Bounded resource allocation after startup (Power of 10 #3): the pool can
// never exceed `schedMaxWorkers` (32) OS threads, full stop.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY `schedMaybeGrow` TAKES THE START RECIPE AS PARAMETERS, NOT AS STATE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `schedStartWorker` takes `entry`/`argArea`/`doneArea`/`handleArea`/
// `startFn` — the platform thread-start recipe — as caller-supplied values,
// and `schedMaybeGrow` forwards them unchanged rather than owning a copy,
// for two independent reasons that both trace to `runtime/sched` building
// `--freestanding` (SPEC §17.6, `tools/build/artifacts.bit`'s libbitrt
// recipe):
//
//   1. A function value (`startFn`'s type) is a `(code, env)` cell that
//      needs a `TypeInfo` descriptor to construct (SPEC §11.10) — "a module
//      that builds one [...] can no longer be emitted --freestanding"
//      (`./workerrun.bit`'s own header, verbatim, about the identical
//      `nowFn`/`pollFn` seam `schedWorkerRun` takes). Referencing a named
//      function to fill a function-typed slot is BUILDING one; this was
//      proved empirically, not assumed — an earlier draft of this file that
//      wrote `schedStartWorker(s, entryOf(growWorkerEntry), ..., someFn)`
//      failed `./make libbitrt` with exactly that diagnostic
//      ("this module allocates on the managed heap"), even though every
//      function involved was a plain top-level one with no captured
//      environment.
//   2. Module-level state cannot HOLD a function value at all (SPEC §11.11,
//      E0082: "only untraced types may live at module scope [...] never a
//      reference") — so even a one-time-configure-then-cache design (the
//      shape `runtime/root/<os>/boot.bit`'s `gSched` uses for the scheduler
//      address) is unavailable here for `startFn` specifically, though it
//      would work for the plain pointers (`entry`/`argArea`/`doneArea`/
//      `handleArea`).
//
// So `schedMaybeGrow` only ever RECEIVES a recipe already built by a
// non-freestanding caller — it builds nothing and stores nothing between
// calls. `runtime/root/<os>/boot.bit`'s `workerBody` already builds the
// identical recipe for the boot-time workers `schedInit` brings up; wiring
// it to also call `schedMaybeGrow` with that same recipe is a follow-up
// ticket, since `boot.bit` sits outside this ticket's file ownership.
// `tests/stress/schedgrowdemand{darwin,linux}` supply their own recipe the
// same way `tests/stress/schedgrowdarwin` already does for `schedStartWorker`
// directly, proving the policy end-to-end with real OS threads.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THE ENQUEUE PATH OBSERVES BUT CANNOT ITSELF START A WORKER
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `schedSpawn` (`./workerrun.bit`) is the scheduler's only path for brand-new
// work — production code reaches it exclusively through `bit_rt_spawn`, one
// call site per OS in `runtime/root/<os>/boot.bit`'s `rtSpawn`, never
// directly from generated code. Hooking the observation there means a
// program that spawns nothing never advances the streak past 0, which is the
// property the ticket asks for: "a program that never has parallel work
// never pays". `schedUnpark`/`schedPollUnpark` also push onto the same
// global queue when they wake a PARKED task, but that is existing work
// resuming, not new work arriving, and is deliberately not observed here.
//
// `schedSpawn` is `@nosplit`, and that is not incidental — its only caller,
// `rtSpawn`, must not be preempted mid-registration, and `@nosplit` is
// transitive: a `@nosplit` function may call only other `@nosplit` functions
// (SPEC §10.3.1). `schedMaybeGrow` cannot be one of those callees: it calls
// `schedStartWorker`, whose body invokes the caller-supplied `startFn` — a
// call THROUGH A VALUE, whose target is not knowable statically, which SPEC
// §10.3.1 rules E0075 (`nosplit_calls_allocating`) unconditionally. So no
// function that (transitively) starts a worker can be reached from
// `schedSpawn` synchronously, and this is a property of `schedStartWorker`'s
// existing, already-landed design (#2592), not something this file can
// route around.
//
// The split this forces: `schedGrowObserve` below is the `@nosplit` half —
// a plain counter, callable from `schedSpawn` — and `schedMaybeGrow` is the
// other half, which cannot be `@nosplit`. This mirrors `./preempt.bit`'s own
// precedent exactly: that file's header states its logic is
// `@nosplit`-clean and still had to leave the actual call site to a later
// ticket, for the identical reason (a `@nosplit` safepoint check may only
// call another `@nosplit` function). Growth's logic cannot even clear that
// first bar, because starting a thread is irreducibly not `@nosplit`.

// Consecutive non-empty checks required before starting one more worker.
// Small on purpose: the ceiling above is what bounds the pool, not this —
// this only bounds how quickly it reacts once real backlog shows up.
export const growStreakThreshold: int = 4

// The `@nosplit` half: called from `schedSpawn`'s own body on every enqueue.
// Plain integer compare, no allocation, no lock beyond the one `gqNonEmpty`
// itself takes none of — it is a racy, lock-free peek by design (see
// `queue.bit`'s own header) — so this never acquires a NEW lock at all,
// which is the stronger form of "no lock beyond the one the enqueue path
// already holds": it takes none.
export @nosplit fn schedGrowObserve(s: *i64) {
  if (!gqNonEmpty(s + scGlobal)) {
    atomicStore(s + scGrowStreak, 0)
    return
  }
  atomicStore(s + scGrowStreak, atomicLoad(s + scGrowStreak) + 1)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE LOCAL COUNTERPART (#3583) — schedSpawn's dqPushBottom branch never
// touches the global queue at all, so schedGrowObserve above never sees
// backlog for the common case (#3264 sends an ordinary in-worker spawn to
// the caller's own ring whenever it has room, and the ring holds 256). The
// bug this closes: growth was unreachable for any CPU-bound program that
// never overflows a single worker's ring.
//
// DESIGN: read depth directly rather than count consecutive observations.
// `schedGrowObserve`'s streak exists because `gqNonEmpty` is a bare boolean
// with no magnitude, so persistence across repeat calls is the only signal
// available and the count is inherently one call behind (the call that
// first makes the queue non-empty cannot itself observe that). The local
// ring's `dqHead`/`dqTail` already give an exact count for free
// (`dqDepth`, `./queue.bit`) — there is no reason to throw that away and
// re-derive persistence the same indirect way. So this checks depth
// directly, once, at the moment a task lands on the ring: `depth` at or
// above `growStreakThreshold` tasks sitting on ONE worker's ring, all
// unclaimed, IS real backlog the instant it is observed, not something
// that needs confirming again next turn.
//
// FALSE POSITIVES: deliberately does nothing below the threshold. A ring
// carrying 1-3 tasks — three short spawns that finish before anyone could
// steal them, say — never touches `scGrowStreak` here at all, so it cannot
// perturb (or reset) whatever `schedGrowObserve`'s own global-queue streak
// is doing elsewhere. Symmetric with the CPU-bound case: a fourth spawn
// landing on top of three still-queued ones is exactly the shape
// `growStreakThreshold` already treats as worth a worker for the global
// queue (`tests/stress/schedgrowdemanddarwin`'s s2 needs its fifth spawn,
// i.e. depth 4 on the queue it fills, to cross the identical threshold) —
// this is the same policy constant applied to a second, independently
// exact source of depth, not a new or looser bar.
//
// Latches to the threshold rather than the raw depth so a pathological
// burst (deque capacity is 256) cannot leave `scGrowStreak` at some huge
// number that then takes many `schedGrowObserve` resets to unwind, and
// leaves whatever is already there untouched below the bar — this can only
// ever raise the streak, never lower one `schedGrowObserve` is mid-building
// from real global-queue backlog.
//
// `@nosplit`, O(1), lock-free: one comparison against an already-computed
// `int` and, on the rare crossing, one atomic store — no loop, no new lock
// beyond the one `dqDepth` itself takes none of.
export @nosplit fn schedGrowObserveLocal(s: *i64, depth: int) {
  if (depth < growStreakThreshold) {
    return
  }
  atomicStore(s + scGrowStreak, growStreakThreshold)
}

// The platform thread-start recipe `schedMaybeGrow`/`schedStartWorker`/
// `schedStart` all take verbatim: caller-owned areas sized for at least
// `schedMaxWorkers` entries, plus the worker entry point and the platform
// `threadStart` value. #2567 grouping this cuts each of the three from 6
// params to 2 (`s`, `launch`) without adding a heap allocation on any path
// that runs today — every construction site is a test/boot fixture that
// already builds this same recipe as five loose locals, so a
// `WorkerLaunch{...}` composite literal there replaces five call-site
// arguments with one, it does not add a new per-call cost. It is declared
// here, never CONSTRUCTED anywhere in this module: `schedMaybeGrow` only
// ever forwards a `launch` it received, matching the "receives, never
// builds" rule the header above already establishes for `startFn` alone —
// this class changes nothing about that; see the header for why building
// the closure `startFn` needs is unavailable to a `--freestanding` module
// regardless of whether it travels as five params or one field.
export class WorkerLaunch {
  entry: *byte,
  argArea: *i64,
  doneArea: *i32,
  handleArea: *i64,
  startFn: (*byte, *byte, *i32) => int,
}

// The other half: starts at most one extra worker, only when the streak
// `schedGrowObserve` has been building reached `growStreakThreshold` AND the
// pool is still below `schedMaxWorkers`. Cannot be `@nosplit` — see the
// header — so a caller outside `runtime/sched` (boot, or a test standing in
// for boot) drives it, supplying the same platform thread-start recipe it
// would give `schedStartWorker` directly.
//
// Resets the streak on every call that reaches the threshold, win or lose: a
// failed `schedStartWorker` (pool already full, or the thread create itself
// failed) must not be retried on literally the next call — the next
// `growStreakThreshold` consecutive non-empty enqueues earn the next
// attempt, exactly as a fresh streak would after a genuinely idle stretch.
export fn schedMaybeGrow(s: *i64, launch: WorkerLaunch): bool {
  if (atomicLoad(s + scGrowStreak) < growStreakThreshold) {
    return false
  }
  atomicStore(s + scGrowStreak, 0)
  if (atomicLoad(s + scNWorkers) >= schedMaxWorkers) {
    return false
  }
  return schedStartWorker(s, launch)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE FREESTANDING-SAFE SPLIT (#3569)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `schedMaybeGrow` above is unusable from `runtime/root/<os>/boot.bit`'s
// `workerBody` run loop for the reason this file's own header already proves
// for the *test* caller's `startFn` argument: naming the platform
// `threadStart` to fill that slot BUILDS a `(code, env)` closure, and
// `boot.bit` emits `--freestanding` (SPEC §17.6) and may not build one either
// — confirmed empirically, not assumed: wiring `schedMaybeGrow` straight into
// `workerBody` fails `./make libbitrt` with "this module allocates on the
// managed heap (a closure value)".
//
// `runtime/thread/threadpool.bit`'s `schedStartArm` solves the identical
// problem for BOOT-TIME bring-up by splitting "arm the slot" from "start the
// thread", so the freestanding caller supplies only a `*byte` and calls its
// own platform `threadStart` directly. `schedGrowArm`/`schedGrowCommit` below
// are that same split for POST-BOOT growth. They cannot simply CALL
// `schedStartArm` — `runtime/sched` may not import `runtime/thread` (the
// reverse import already exists, and the cycle is E0044) — so the two-word
// `{worker address, done-word address}` block is built inline here, in the
// same shape, exactly as `schedStartWorker` already does below for the same
// reason (see its own header).
//
// LOCK DISCIPLINE — THE PART THIS TICKET CALLS OUT AS THE HARD PART.
// `schedStartWorker` holds `scRegLock` across its ENTIRE claim-init-start
// sequence, including the `startFn` call, releasing only after `scNWorkers`
// is published — see its header for why: publishing the count only on
// success, under one lock held throughout, is what stops two concurrent
// growers from ever claiming the same slot. Splitting "arm" from "start"
// into two separate Bit functions cannot copy that lock span verbatim (a
// spinlock cannot be acquired in one function and released in another
// safely UNLESS the two calls are known to run back-to-back on the same
// thread with nothing but the platform `threadStart` call in between) — but
// that is exactly the shape here: `workerBody` calls `schedGrowArm`, then
// (only on a non-zero return) calls `threadStart` directly, then calls
// `schedGrowCommit`, all on the SAME OS thread, with no other Bit call in
// between. So the invariant `schedStartWorker` relies on is preserved
// exactly: `scRegLock` is held from the moment a slot is claimed until the
// moment the new worker count (or the abandonment of a failed start) is
// published, it is just held ACROSS a module boundary instead of within one
// function body. Two workers hitting the threshold concurrently still
// serialise on this same lock, in the same order they would have inside
// `schedStartWorker`: the second blocks in `schedGrowArm`'s `spinAcquire`
// until the first's `schedGrowCommit` releases it, and by then `scNWorkers`
// already reflects the first grower's outcome, so the second claims the
// NEXT slot rather than the same one.
//
// `schedGrowArm` ALSO refuses once `scStopping` is set, checked under the
// same lock as the ceiling: without this, a worker could still be mid-grow
// (lock held, about to call `threadStart`) at the instant `schedShutdown`
// runs on the boot thread, and the newly started worker would never be
// reaped by `boot()`'s join loop (its `started` bound is read once, after
// `schedShutdown`). Refusing under the lock means that by the time a
// caller's `schedShutdown` has run, every `schedGrowArm` that has not yet
// taken the lock will see `scStopping != 0` and decline, and any grow that
// is already past the lock will finish publishing to `scNWorkers` before
// `schedShutdown`'s caller can observe a stable count (see
// `schedFinalWorkerCount` below, which reads `scNWorkers` under the
// identical lock for exactly this reason).
//
// Never call `schedGrowCommit` except after a `schedGrowArm` call that
// returned non-zero — doing so releases a lock this thread does not hold.

// The arming half. On success, initializes and arms worker slot
// `scNWorkers`, leaves `scRegLock` HELD, and returns the `*byte` argument a
// platform `threadStart(entryOf(workerBody), arg, done)` call needs — the
// same `{worker address, done-word address}` block `schedStartArm` builds
// for boot-time bring-up, unpacked with `taskAt(int(arg))` exactly as
// `workerBody` already unpacks its own start argument. Returns a genuine
// nil `*byte` (address 0, never a live block address) and touches the lock
// not at all, or only transiently, in every case that is NOT "go start a
// worker": streak below threshold (lock never taken), pool already at
// `schedMaxWorkers`, or shutdown already signalled (lock taken and released
// before returning).
export @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_grow_arm")
fn schedGrowArm(s: *i64, argArea: *i64, doneArea: *i32): *byte {
  let bp: *byte = nil
  if (atomicLoad(s + scGrowStreak) < growStreakThreshold) {
    return bp
  }
  atomicStore(s + scGrowStreak, 0)

  spinAcquire(lockOf(s, scRegLock))
  let i = atomicLoad(s + scNWorkers)
  if (i >= schedMaxWorkers || atomicLoad(s + scStopping) != 0) {
    spinRelease(lockOf(s, scRegLock))
    return bp
  }

  schedInitWorkerSlot(s, i)
  let block = argArea + i * 2
  *block = int(schedWorkerAt(s, i))
  let done = doneArea + i
  *(block + 1) = int(done)
  // 1 is `runtime/thread`'s `threadRunning` sentinel, mirrored rather than
  // imported — same reason as `schedStartWorker`'s identical line below.
  atomicStore(done, i32(1))
  return bp + int(block)
}

// The commit half. Must be called exactly once for every `schedGrowArm`
// call that returned a non-zero `*byte`, with the `int` result of the
// caller's own `threadStart(...)` call in between and nothing else. Stores
// the handle and publishes the grown count on success; on failure
// (`handle == 0`) abandons the slot without publishing. Either way releases
// `scRegLock`, so `schedGrowArm`'s claim is always matched by exactly one
// release.
export @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_grow_commit")
fn schedGrowCommit(s: *i64, handleArea: *i64, handle: int): bool {
  if (handle == 0) {
    spinRelease(lockOf(s, scRegLock))
    return false
  }
  let i = atomicLoad(s + scNWorkers)
  *(handleArea + i) = handle
  atomicStore(s + scNWorkers, i + 1)
  spinRelease(lockOf(s, scRegLock))
  return true
}

// A plain, racy `atomicLoad(s + scNWorkers)` is NOT enough for `boot()`'s own
// reap-loop bound, and the gap is not cosmetic: `boot()` closes the netpoller
// fd right after its join loop, specifically because a still-running worker's
// `pollDrain` on a closed fd is a use-after-close, not a leak (see
// `boottail.bit`'s step 8b). A worker `schedGrowArm` armed but had not yet
// published to `scNWorkers` at the instant a racy read ran would be silently
// left out of that reap loop and could still be mid-`pollDrain` when the fd
// closes. Taking `scRegLock` here closes the gap: called ONLY after the
// caller's `schedShutdown(s)`, any `schedGrowArm` that has not yet reached
// the lock will see `scStopping != 0` there and decline (see `schedGrowArm`'s
// header), and any grow already past the lock finishes publishing before
// this can acquire it — either way, the count this returns is exactly the
// set of workers `boot()` must join before it may close anything shared.
export @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_final_worker_count")
fn schedFinalWorkerCount(s: *i64): int {
  spinAcquire(lockOf(s, scRegLock))
  let n = atomicLoad(s + scNWorkers)
  spinRelease(lockOf(s, scRegLock))
  return n
}

// TEMPORARY INSTRUMENTATION (#3726) — packs the grow streak and the current
// worker count into one lock-free word so `tryGrowPool` can trace both without
// taking `scRegLock`. Removed in the following commit.
export @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_grow_peek") fn schedGrowPeek(s: *i64): int {
  return atomicLoad(s + scGrowStreak) * 64 + atomicLoad(s + scNWorkers)
}
